{"kind":"request","text":"Hugh's explicit instruction, 2026-10-09: send planner this writeup and ask for detailed tasks for builder to implement. This consolidates the agreed counting-app refinement and invitation journey from the current discussion.\n\nPRODUCT DIRECTION\nIndependent agents discover and join a shared counting room from any location. There is a variable population, not a fixed three/four-agent model; three and four are recording choreography only. Adopt a practical membership/admission limit to prevent flooding. Members can remain enrolled while active or inactive for counting. Each agent has its own identity, local execution loop and speech capability.\n\nReplace centrally assigned speaker rotation as the principal interaction with agents making commitments: an eligible agent proposes “I commit to speak N”; Artroom sequences and judges proposals under declared rules; one accepted commitment establishes responsibility; competing proposals can be refused normally under contention. Only after verifying its accepted recorded commitment does that agent speak. This demonstrates promises and workroom sequencing, rather than only logging speech after the fact.\n\nTwo concrete agent implementations share this application contract:\n- A macOS command-line/MCP-connected agent runs local say through its authorized local execution tool.\n- An agent running in a web page uses local browser speech synthesis.\nMCP supplies room interaction; it is not itself a speech or execution runtime. Do not run all speech centrally or have the operator sign as the independent agents.\n\nDELIVERABLE\nWrite a dated, concrete task breakdown and issue detailed builder requests through the existing workroom workflow. Each task must name its exact source area, inputs/dependencies, selected contract, implementation boundary, acceptance witnesses and review/landing treatment. Put shared design decisions in one coherent dated note or the appropriate successor counting/interface note; avoid conflicting duplicate plans. Builder should implement selected decisions, not choose essential product semantics from a menu.\n\nReconcile full counting request 4073e7561b47b3b13aaceb616d9fc3d67478af5e, its C0/F1/W1/U1 successors, durability infrastructure 578edf653772749b5fa9b47f6782f6db4d0ab720, live developer interface 785a544715e7bc45e4e5e35edc7bbe78e9f289dc and existing invitation/enrollment owners. Preserve current critical correctness/integration priorities and the natural demo. This refinement changes turn acquisition; it does not abandon native declared coordination, replay, independent identities, automatic updates, hosted/audio acceptance or the complete earlier counting delivery.\n\n1. DECLARED COMMITMENT-BASED COUNTING\nChoose a minimal versioned successor counting declaration and exact native act/result names. Suggested logical sequence:\nobserve open next number → propose commitment → reconcile/verify recorded acceptance → speak locally → report fulfillment → release next number.\nKeep at most one outstanding speaking commitment in the initial application: ordering multiple promises alone does not prevent overlapping audio. The scope must verify the exact number, eligible member, expected revisions, current run/turn identity, deadline and absence of another outstanding commitment. Successful commitment is itself recorded before speech; fulfillment is a later record linked to that promise. Preserve replayable eligibility/selection basis.\n\nDistinguish refused competing proposals (no established promise) from failure/expiry of an accepted promise (an obligation existed). Do not promise that all refused attempts become journal entries if the actual admission contract does not retain them. The stage should present contention as ordinary competition, not a visible failure parade.\n\nThe word round robin in the discussion was exploratory. First-successful-claim is not fair round robin and can favor low-latency agents. Select and state a small eligibility/fairness rule. The assistant suggested excluding the last successful speaker from the immediately following turn while multiple members are active; that is a candidate, not separately adopted by Hugh, and does not guarantee fairness to all participants. Avoid recreating a hidden central speaker scheduler. Explain the chosen fairness and starvation limits.\n\nChoose precise behavior for inactive/departed speaker, explicit failure, deadline, pause/reset, no eligible participants and target completion. Specify whether expiry pauses or records failure and opens a replacement claim for the same number. Lost fulfillment can leave actual speech uncertain; do not equate missing evidence with silence. Retain unresolved original requests even after cancellation/reset. An expired/replaced promise's late callback cannot complete its replacement. The count and UI must distinguish promised number from fulfilled number.\n\nKeep application-specific rules in the declaration and common interpreter, not in Core, a relay or the browser's coordinator. Preserve old pins/identities and define explicit migration/new-app establishment for the successor.\n\n2. VARIABLE MEMBERSHIP AND ACTIVE/INACTIVE PARTICIPATION\nSeparate native room membership/authority, durable counting eligibility and volatile connection/session presence. Inactive counting members remain enrolled; disconnected does not mean revoked, and a socket heartbeat does not make a durable active participant. Provide explicit reversible activation/deactivation with clear self/controller authority and consequences for an outstanding commitment. Rejoining should not silently manufacture a new identity or erase responsibility.\n\nChoose a practical bound on enrolled members/admission, not just active speakers. Current C0 caps joined participants at eight; that does not by itself bound enrollment or outstanding invitations. Define how pending invitations, repeated joins, inactive members and removals consume/release capacity. Reuse actual native capacity/admission mechanisms; do not add an unrelated unbounded public registration system. Agent lists and allocation must be dynamic rather than hard-coded to three/four.\n\n3. ONE INVITATION URL FOR BROWSER AND CLI\nWe have an existing one-time invitation payload and native join act:\n- packages/cli/src/commands.ts: Link, LINK, invite, linkOf, join; custom artroom-invite: payload carries service, repository/membership refs, invitation ID/secret, handle and membership pin.\n- CLI saves its exact enrollment request and key privately before sending and resumes the same request.\n- packages/page/src/data.ts: admittedInvitation, enrollmentAssociation, joinRoom; Page currently pastes the invitation, makes a key and joins. Inspected Page accepts membership@1/@2 only and does not durably retain the exact Join envelope.\n- Counting browser startup currently requires a pre-enrolled key file and public binding JSON; that manual transfer must be replaced by the invitation journey.\nThese are inspected source observations, not a hosted acceptance claim. Check current successors and exact deployed versions before designing.\n\nImplement an ordinary HTTPS invitation URL accepted directly by both browser and artroom join. Choose an exact route/encoding with the secret confined to a client-handled fragment or an equivalently justified private mechanism; bind to the configured service and full membership/app identities. A browser landing page, CLI and MCP adapter use shared bounded parsing/validation and the same native enrollment semantics. Merely opening/reading the URL must not consume the invitation; enrollment occurs only through the signed native act. Do not fetch arbitrary origins/redirects or trust URL metadata as authorization.\n\nBrowser journey: open URL → show intended identity/room and Join action → create/retain this agent's local signer → privately retain exact enrollment request → verify accepted membership/session → discover the counting app → choose/arm browser voice → become active.\nCLI journey: artroom join '\u003csame HTTPS URL\u003e' → locally retain its own key and exact enrollment request → verify membership/session → discover counting app → start its counting execution loop using say, optionally through MCP/local tools.\nExact new commands and MCP names are planner choices, with executable examples in the handoff; do not document proposed commands as already available.\n\nResolve newer counting-factory membership cohort compatibility in the shared parser/client/session path without newest aliases or weakening pin checks. Add browser recovery on the same semantic basis as CLI: lost reply/reload resumes or settles the original key/envelope, never signs a replacement blindly. No private key copying from CLI into browser and no shared operator identity. Joining membership and activating counting are distinct actions; grants and application pin/address discovery must make the second one usable automatically.\n\nA personal one-time invitation for a named member is different from a reusable public room entrance. Select the concrete demo policy: independently issued named links are a supported starting point. If the public room URL is intended to let any visiting agent obtain an invitation, specify and task the bounded authorized issuance/request process and naming/admission decision. Never make one personal secret a reusable enrollment credential. State any deferred public-admission capability plainly.\n\n4. TWO INDEPENDENT AGENT RUNNERS, ONE SMALL API\nUse the selected live TypeScript application handle/discover/read/invoke/observe contract and the convergent HTTP/MCP interfaces; coordinate with existing W1 rather than write another watch stack. Build a reusable logical agent loop with bounded contention retries/backoff and one small local speech adapter. A losing agent rereads/waits; unknown commit outcomes reconcile the original signed request before any speech. A view/head change does not settle that request.\n\nFor macOS use local say; report successful process completion, and handle nonzero exit/cancellation as defined application outcomes. MCP invocation must operate under that agent's legitimate key and host's available local execution tools. Keep platform/tool permissions explicit; do not add operator impersonation.\nFor browser use speechSynthesis with explicit user gesture/voice readiness; bind end/error/cancel callbacks to the exact accepted promise and local audio attempt. Both adapters report authenticated local completion, not proof that all listeners heard sound. No audible exactly-once claim across process/device crash, and no blind replay on unknown acknowledgment. Keep private completion/request custody, exact recovery and current native authority/session windows.\n\nRemote agents need repeatable setup/discovery and automatic authorized updates. Artroom remains one authoritative counting scope reachable globally; equal worldwide latency or synchronized acoustic delivery is not promised. Show the same app contract working through the browser/TypeScript and CLI/MCP paths.\n\n5. STAGE, VERIFICATION AND BUILDER TASKS\nAdapt the stage to show who promised the current number, pending/recorded commitment, speaking, fulfillment and active/inactive participants. Keep inspectors/replay secondary, setup short and ordinary contention unobtrusive. Use a dynamic roster and both agent environments. A convincing recording can show three initial agents, another joining, activity changes, and continued counting without pretending those numbers are fixed limits.\n\nMinimum acceptance at meaningful native/integration boundaries:\n- One HTTPS invitation is redeemable by either chosen client; reading it does not consume it. Separate agents use separate invitations/identities.\n- Concurrent redemption/capacity/expiry and lost reply/reload retain exact enrollment identity; supported counting cohort works end to end.\n- Enrollment yields actual counting grants, discovery/session and usable activation without copying keys or binding JSON.\n- Concurrent claims establish exactly one current recorded promise; losers do not speak.\n- Unknown acceptance is reconciled before speech; fulfillment binds the exact promise; duplicate/late callbacks and reset cannot advance a replacement.\n- Inactivity/departure/expiry obey the selected declared policy; no-participant cases remain bounded.\n- Native journal replay explains promises, fulfillment/failure and eligibility; reactivation retains recorded state.\n- Real local say and real browser speech each fulfill accepted commitments with automatic peer updates. Report acoustic uncertainty honestly.\n- One independent remotely located agent participates through the same public interface; measure acknowledgment/peer updates separately from speech latency only within a bounded rehearsal.\n\nUse docs/testing.md: focused meaningful witnesses while implementing, one coordinated final gate before source review, no mutation sweeps/repeated whole suites/checkout tool installations. Source-only or fake speech previews are not hosted/audio proof.\n\nREQUESTED PLANNER RESPONSE\nReturn the selected contract, practical limits and invitation/admission policy, followed by concrete sequenced builder tasks with owners/dependencies, exact files/symbols, test boundaries and full delivery criteria. File the builder commissions and name their IDs. Keep design, source validation and real hosted/audio evidence distinguishable, while carrying the intended complete experience through implementation.","body":{"conditions":"Write a dated concrete counting/invitation task breakdown and issue detailed builder implementation requests. Incorporate recorded commitments before speech, subsequent fulfillment, contention/recovery, variable bounded membership and reversible counting activity, shared HTTPS invitation/enrollment/discovery for browser and CLI, local macOS say and browser speech adapters, convergent TypeScript/MCP interfaces and native hosted/audio acceptance. Resolve exact fairness/expiry/admission decisions, cohort compatibility and browser enrollment recovery; reconcile existing counting/factory/watch/interface owners and preserve complete counting delivery.","target_head":"8479057c1ce50f01a5de488c2f6c029b45be65e7","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}